WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:03.010
Film Courage: Is outlining a must?

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:04.390
Scott Myers, DePaul University Professor/Screenwriter/Creator
Gointothestory.blcklst(dot)com: Again, ever

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:06.960
writer is different.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:14.020
I would say if you have any pretensions of
working in TV, yes.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:19.750
No project goes to script (no episode) unless
they break it in prep and that typically involves

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.000
either a beat sheet or an outline.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.730
On the feature side of things if you’re
writing on spec and you’ve got 12 months

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.980
and you want to with Fade In: and see where
it goes, that’s okay.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.250
But I definitely recommend outlining.

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:38.289
If you’ve not outlined, you should outline
at least once and see what that process is

00:00:38.289 --> 00:00:39.289
like.

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:45.960
The way I approach it that I think enlivens
the experience is as I said before we work

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.479
with the characters.

00:00:47.479 --> 00:00:53.010
So week one protagonist character treatment,
ask seven key questions of the protagonist

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:58.059
so you are engaging them and really digging
down into who they are.

00:00:58.059 --> 00:01:02.319
Questions like what do they fear most, that’s
an interesting question.

00:01:02.319 --> 00:01:05.510
Why does this story have to happen to this
character at this time.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:09.000
Think about their sort of place in life.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.880
Second week we do brainstorming, some of those
exercises I was talking about, questionnaires,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:20.530
biographies, these direct engagement exercises,
interviews, monologues and steams of consciousness.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:24.970
Third week we now move to plot so we talk
about what is the beginning of the story.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.420
What is the end of Act one?

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.890
What is the end of Act 2?

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:28.890
What is the end of the story?

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.910
Just to get a sense of those four primary
plot line points.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:34.860
But we are also identifying subplots.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.750
My principle there is SubPlot = A Relationship.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.740
So whatever character you’ve come up with,
the primary characters in your story then

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:49.180
start to look at them in terms of their relationships
and if you can work with character archetypes,

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.280
that’s very helpful.

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:51.280
Oh?

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.000
This is a protagonist-attractor relationship,
this is a protagonist-mentor relationship,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.130
etcetera.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:01.380
And so you start to think in terms of subplots
and in week four we do the psychological domain

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:06.160
and we talk about these four theme line movements,
disunity, deconstruction, reconstruction,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.160
unity.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.869
For the first few weeks it really is focused
on character and by the time you get to the

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:16.209
last stage where we are breaking out 3X5 index
cards and doing that whole thing you know

00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:22.470
that you’ve spent so much time with the
characters and generated so much content,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:27.540
there is a vibrancy to it, there’s an aliveness
to it, there’s a vitality to it.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:32.201
It’s not just literally plugging in this-to-that
and this-to-that and this-to-that, you are

00:02:32.201 --> 00:02:37.239
really seeing the story unfold and you are
sensing how the characters are involved in

00:02:37.239 --> 00:02:40.989
the protagonist’s psychological journey
and their arc.

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:45.139
So I would say it’s not mandatory to do
an outline.

00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:49.590
I highly recommend it.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:55.170
If you feel that it is better for you to write
a treatment version of it, a more literary

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.560
thing, that’s fine, too.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:57.989
Or a beat sheet.

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:02.129
But I do outlines and they can run to 25-30
pages long like single-space because I put

00:03:02.129 --> 00:03:04.590
everything in there, like what is the central
conflict?

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.280
Why are these characters, lines of dialogue
I’ve heard.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.950
How am I going to get in-and-out of the scenes
(transitions to the next scene).

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:11.499
I put it all there.

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:15.019
I would strongly encourage people to do an
outline, yes.

00:03:15.019 --> 00:03:17.700
Particularly if they want to work in TV they
just absolutely have to.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:22.200
Film Courage: Let’s say someone does not
want to do an outline and you say “Alright,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.200
that’s fine.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.359
That’s your style even though I highly recommend
it.”

00:03:25.359 --> 00:03:30.680
Then you read their script and see that it
could have benefited from them taking time,

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.669
seeing the story a bigger picture.

00:03:32.669 --> 00:03:33.669
What is your answer to this?

00:03:33.669 --> 00:03:35.230
Scott: Well here is the thing.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:39.349
I’d say nine times out of ten I’m not
going to get that script because they are

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:43.829
going to crash and burn by page 50 or 60 because
they don’t know where they are going.

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:45.510
Anybody can write a first act.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:52.689
You’ve got to establish the key characters,
the story universe, the central conflict,

00:03:52.689 --> 00:03:59.769
the atmosphere and tone, something happens
(the inciting incident or the call to adventure)

00:03:59.769 --> 00:04:03.200
and then something propels them off into this
new world right?

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:09.870
Well so you can probably (off that energy)
go Okay the next 10-15 pages…and then maybe

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.400
5 more…but I don’t know where I’m going?

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.950
Maybe I better figure this out?

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:18.450
Okay I’ll write another 10-15 but well…that’s
stupid…that doesn’t really work.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:19.450
Oh wow?

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:24.440
And so people will get…I think the number
one reason why people do not finish a script

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.690
is because they don’t know where they are
going?

00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:29.760
If you do an outline, you do know where you
are going.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.170
Like you are familiar with Washington D.C.,
there is the Chesapeake Bay Bridge tunnel

00:04:35.170 --> 00:04:37.010
(I do a blog post on this, it’s pretty funny).

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:41.480
The Chesapeake Bay Bridge tunnel extends from
Virginia Beach up to the Delmarva Peninsula

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.290
which is Delaware, Maryland and Virginia.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:48.500
And it’s long, it’s like 20-some odd miles
long.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.480
So you’ve got this span which is a bridge
and then there is a tunnel for like a mile-and-a-half

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:56.560
which is creepy enough because you are under
the Atlantic Ocean in this tunnel.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.780
Then you come up and there is a mid-part of
the bridge and then there is another tunnel

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.260
and another part of the bridge.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:07.440
That middle part of the bridge which is about
10 miles out, if you go during the day, you

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.890
cannot see land anywhere.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:10.160
There is no land.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:13.750
You are on a bridge in the middle of the ocean.

00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.500
If you have an outline at least you know where
you’re going and you just keep going and

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:26.040
going because that middle of the story (Act
2) can be so discombobulating sometimes and

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.840
so you know where you’re going because you’ve
got the thing broken because you’re trusting

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.540
you are going to come out the other end.

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:39.371
But yes absolutely, I’ve read scripts where
oh gosh, they didn’t know the structure

00:05:39.371 --> 00:05:42.300
or structures or a mess.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:46.650
But if there is something there and the characters
are vibrant then yes, we can work around that.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:55.670
Structure is important absolutely for screenplays
but there are ways to get to that.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:06:03.580
The characters who are alive and vibrant and
compelling, that’s really the harder part

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.050
I think in some respects.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:11.530
Film Courage: You just triggered a memory
when I was a kid and being afraid to have

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:14.160
my grandparents drive me over that bridge.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:17.800
Maybe this is why I’m afraid of bridges.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:23.170
There was the subconscious coming out…see
you brought it out…I forgot all about this.

